A||||58||Nashco||Larger injectors and how to do them....||||1||
Z||000000||Nashco||11-19-2001||04:07 AM||||Well, I scoured through the search engines for a while, and couldn't find any definitive information on how to go about doing larger injectors.<P>I'm curious not only for myself, but for a friend with a Beretta.  He has asked everbody on beretta.net, and they don't know anything.  Obviously, our knowledge here at the forum is immense, as I've never asked a question without getting lots of help  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  So, here goes nothing.<P>1) What is necessary to use larger injectors?  I know that a lot of people go with larger injectors in a 3.4 swap, and many use an adjustable fuel pressure regulator to tune the engine to properly.<P>2) What is the stock rating of our injectors, and what is the recommended upgrade size?<P>3) Where can we get the better injectors, new and used?  Are there any vehicles that used them stock, or are they only available in performance applications?<P>4) Is there a noticeable power gain using the larger injectors and adjustable fuel pressure regulator?  Is it necessary to tune the PROM to get better performance, or only to maximize it?<P>5) How much does the adjustable regulator costs, and where can we get one?<P>Here's the Beretta part.  My friend has a Beretta that was originally equipped with a 3100, but now uses a 3400.  They use the same injectors, and he wants to upgrade for more performance.  I believe that it uses the same style/size injectors as our car, but I'm not sure.  It seems that multiport injectors seem to share these same dimensions and plug style across many makes and models.  Click on this&gt; <A HREF="http://home1.gte.net/jbnash/fiero/injector.jpg" TARGET=_blank>picture</A> &lt;to see the measurements of his injectors.  Is this the same as our injectors?  If somebody has one that they could take pics of, or even just measure for comparison and let us know, that'd be great.  I got to thinking, even if you don't want bigger injectors, this would be good information to have.  We could go get a set from the junkyard and replace our worn out ones with some low mileage ones for a cheap fix, if they'll work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>This isn't the first time the beretta.net hasn't been able to help him out.  My friend has been thoroughly impressed at how much knowledge goes through this forum, just as I have.<P>I'm not ready to do this mod yet, not until I get my 3.4 installed.  After I get it installed and broken in, then I'll start modifying the fuel setup.  Hopefully we can get some good information all in one place to make it easier to figure out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'm open to any and all opinions, experiences, and information.  Thanks for your help everybody  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>*edited picture URL*<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||1
