Z||000003||1FST2M6||11-19-2001||11:09 AM||||STOCK 87-95 mustang 19lb/hr injectors.. will support over 250hp. clip right on and DO NOT require an e-prom change. your current ones are 14+lb/hr the Tomoco over priced units are only 17.3lb/hr. baretta 3.1 units are 16.7. (stock injector infomation from mitchel-on-demand 01 DVD)||||reg||1
Z||000004||olliver||11-19-2001||11:44 AM||||Ok let's get rid of this misconception about larger injectors.<P>First of all... Unless you are going to some form of supercharging/turbocharging, there is absolutely no need to install "larger" injectors into a 2.8L engine.  <P>This is how injectors work.<P>With every second revolution of the crank the injector fires.  This firing is reffered to as injector pulse width.  Now, with a stock engine and stock injectors the engineers have calculated that the pulse width is optimal.  <BR>The formula for the amount of flow is equall to Pulse width X Flow rate of the injector<P>This gives you a quantity of fuel released during every firing of the injector.  This quantity matches the amount of air coming into the engine to give you the magic 12.5 - 14.5 : 1 fuel/air ratio.<P>Now, you install a set of mustang 19lb/h injectors.  The release more fuel with the same pulse width as stock injectors.  Now your fuel/air ratio is going to be like 15-15.5 : 1 the first thing the ecm is going to do is shorten the pulse width.  So now instead of having like a 2.5 ms pulse width with stock injectors, it's going to be like 2.0 ms.  In the end you are still going be supplying the engine with exact same amount of fuel.<P>The moral of the story is....<BR>Don't waste your damn money on "larger" injectors.  If you are going to anything with your injectors get the rebuilt and blueprinted.  <P>Thank you<BR>Olliver||||reg||1
Z||000005||olliver||11-19-2001||11:48 AM||||this relates to any engine size as well.  If you are not supplying considerably larger amounts of air, (enough that the stock injectors cannot release enough fuel within the maximum pulse width allowed by the ecm)...<P>THEN YOU ARE WASTING YOUR MONEY!!!<P>And there is no "if and's or but's about it"||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||11-19-2001||12:19 PM||||Point taken that "bigger" injectors don't do anything for a stock motor, but what if NOS is helping the car out?  Also, forced induction is a large possibility with my car, more than likely a turbo, in the long run.  If I can have this injector monkey off my back before I do it, that would be ideal <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Trav, have you installed different fuel stuff on your car?  How have you compensated for the NOS and intake mods (before the dual intake)?  Would an adjustable fuel pressure regulator be sufficient?<P>Thanks for your help so far guys!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||11-19-2001||01:34 PM||||have you ever hooked up an injector duty cycle monitor to the stock injectors of our cars?  after a $210 refurbishing and blueprinting from RC Engineering they are still above 80% with a larger TB, headers and 2.5" exhaust. i put (too large) 36#/hr units in and the duty cycle never past 30% but spraying i was at 55% (it wouldn't idle for crap but WOT spraying ROCKED) (not having the milli-seconds in front of me i won't quote time.) i'm running 19s now and even n/a i've droped to only 55% too high a cycle and the inj. can freeze up, causing failure with a  misfire or dead cylinder.  yes you are putting in the same amount of fuel but the inj's are doing less work. leaving you more room for modification.. EGR block off... timing advance, spray, forced induction. or on a <B>larger displacement swap</B>. which is what he was asking about  a 3.1 or 3.4 will max out a stock injector new or with 80k miles on it.. stock units don't have the oriface size to move the needed fuel.  just increasing the fuel pressure will cause more problems.. effecting the injectors spray pattern also causing the fuel pump to become ineffecent and heat up which will lead to failure.<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||SKIDMARK||11-19-2001||01:57 PM||||So I'm upgrading to a 3.4 and should probably use the 3.4 injectors, especially if I plan on a turbo in the near future, right? The larger displacement will demand more air/fuel mixture pushing the stock 2.8 injectors to their limits. If I stuff more air in the combustion chamber via the turbo, I will need more fuel to match the increase in air and thus achieve stociometric bliss (14.5:1). Even larger injectors would cause the above mentioned idle problems because the ECM can not adjust the pulse width far enough to compensate at idle. Does this all make sense or am I on the wrong track here?<BR>Edit:<BR>By the way, I have both 2.8 and 3.4 injectors and will try to photo and measure them by tomorrow and will post here if possible.<p>[This message has been edited by SKIDMARK (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Nashco||11-19-2001||02:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>have you ever hooked up an injector duty cycle moniton to the stock injectors of our cars?  after a $210 refurbishing and blueprinting from RC Engineering they are still above 80% with a larger TB, headers and 2.5" exhaust. i put (too large) 36#/hr units in and the duty cycle never past 30% but spraying i was at 55% (it wouldn't idle for crap but WOT spraying ROCKED) (not having the milli-seconds in front of me i won't quote time.) i'm running 19s now and even n/a i've droped to only 55% too high a cycle and the inj. can freeze up, causing failure with a  misfire or dead cylinder.  yes you are putting in the same amount of fuel but the inj's are doing less work. leaving you more room for modification.. EGR block off... timing advance, spray, forced induction. or on a [b]larger displacement swap</B>. which is what he was asking about  a 3.1 or 3.4 will max out a stock injector new or with 80k miles on it.. stock units don't have the oriface size to move the needed fuel.  just increasing the fuel pressure will cause more problems.. effecting the injectors spray pattern also causing the fuel pump to become ineffecent and heat up which will lead to failure.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks Trav, these things are the kind of info I'm looking for.<P>So you say raising fuel pressure won't change anything positively, is that with stock or any injectors?  How do you recommend tweaking fuel settings after larger injectors: fuel pressure regulator, tuned EPROM, or both?<P>I hate to even ask anything like this, as it may be ridiculous, but is it possible to install resistors inline to tweak injector timing?  It would effect the entire RPM range, obviously, so this seems like it would be useless (just bring things back to factory settings).<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FST2M6||11-19-2001||03:34 PM||||raising the fuel pressure does help a bit.. but theres a point of diminishing return for every modification you can make. i guess i should have clarified that.. but it's just a patch not a fix. a rising rate fuel pressue regulator is used a lot on aftermarket turbo systems.. BUT.. on all their instructions they tell you "a good addition to this system is ##lb/hr injectors... our cars not being force fed for a bit more $ than a rising rate get an Apexi S-AFC it will adjust your fuel curve with out destroying the spray patterens on your injectors. BBBAD GT showed me the error of my ways.. 70+psi on 36# units was wasting more gas then i was making up in time spraying nitrous. after a little messaging 19# stock mustang units have been the best for MY 2.8 applications. (both nitrous fed) but obiously you can't run nitrous all the time.. it idles fine in traffic, gets 16-20mpg when i'm commuting and about 8 when i'm spraying.. duty cycle goes up AND the additional fuel i'm spraying just drains the tank really quick. i'd say for a push rod 2.8-3.4 19# units would be fine. enough the computer can compensate and enough so that your not taxing you inj or the rest of the system. my air fuel ratios at WOT (no spray) is about 13.8-14.0 yeah a bit rich but still safe. an engine unless under really expensive management will rarely run at 14.7:1 or stoichiometric perfection. a MOTEC or TECII  both over $2000 plus install and requires constant tuning to stay in tune. and spraying i'm runnin ga SWEET 12.8 and fattening up (still working on that) above 3500 to a soggy 11.3.<P>my findings are 19s... for push rods. i've got no experiance with 3.4 TDC engines so go fish if you looking for info on them. the 19s are cheaper then the Tomoco units from the fiero store, and better.   ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Alex4mula||11-19-2001||03:46 PM||||You can get 8 new Accel 19#s from Jegs/Summit for about $220.<BR> Is there any easy way to measure/deduce duty cycle with a scanner? I'm still not convinced that I need them in my 3.4.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
