Z||000013||Nashco||11-20-2001||12:44 AM||||Alright, things are starting to come together...couple more questions.<P>So, a starter is to get the 19# injectors and an adjustable fuel regulator.  After you've got that paid for and installed, a S-AFC unit from Apexi is suggested for more tuning.<P>What about skipping the adjustable regulator and just getting the S-AFC?  Will that take care of all your fuel control woes?  The S-AFC doesn't look to cost as much as I was expecting (300-350 bucks), and a fuel pressure regulator is about a hundred bucks, right?  The new model Apexi S-AFC with the cool VFD screen would be a nice addition to the car anyway, just for cool factor <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I'm really surprised at how cheap electronic engine controls are getting nowadays.  I know you get what you pay for, as there are some awesome systems (in the 2k range, as Trav said)...but I wouldn't be able to put that to use with my engine now anyway.<P>SKIDMARK:  Thanks, the pic will be appreciated.  My friend is eager to find out if he can replace his injectors like we do <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>This is some really good info, thanks for your help so far guys.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||1FST2M6||11-20-2001||08:37 AM||||i've got a nickle under the diaphram in my rebuilt stock FPR and the S-AFC. running 19s. the stock rebuilt kit from NAPA is like $10. the s-afc will allow you to set the duty cycle in a given RPM range. <P>as for duty cycle on a scanner.. yeah.. the shop (EP) has a Matco Realty, a Snap-on Commander and a Palm Pilot with the right plug ($19 from the snap on man) that will all read the ecu on my car real time and print out what it's doing. those are the scanners i've used. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Nashco||11-20-2001||09:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>i've got a nickle under the diaphram in my rebuilt stock FPR and the S-AFC. running 19s. the stock rebuilt kit from NAPA is like $10. the s-afc will allow you to set the duty cycle in a given RPM range. <P>as for duty cycle on a scanner.. yeah.. the shop (EP) has a Matco Realty, a Snap-on Commander and a Palm Pilot with the right plug ($19 from the snap on man) that will all read the ecu on my car real time and print out what it's doing. those are the scanners i've used. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The Snap On scanner reads real time alone right?  Or were you saying that AND the palm does it?<P>If it only takes a palm and the plug from the Snap On, I can't imagine their scanner would be selling much longer at five times the price <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I have a buddy that <B>loves</B> doing weird stuff with his palm, and I have a snap on scanner on the way in the mail, so either way is good for me!<P>Just to check, you're still running the stock ECM, right Trav?<P>As a side note, has anybody fiddled with Holley fuel injection stuff?  Somebody at work is selling a Commander 950 multi-port setup that I could use on my 4.3 for easy tuning; I'm trying to decide if I should get a sy/ty computer and tweak the chips, or if I should just get this Holley setup for all the basic N/A tuning.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||1FST2M6||11-20-2001||09:49 AM||||the head fabricator at the shop just got a palm.. and it reads my car.. yep it's running a stock ECU.  for under $300 the palm is the eway to go. the Reality that the shop owner has was nearly 3k!!! and johns "little toy" does just as much for WAY less. <BR>----edit<BR>the 950 is the way to go.. cheaper then a haltech easier to use.. we've got one in a turbo 240z, a nitrous fed LS integra and turbo integra and a s/c focus.. it allows you to find the magical hidden HP you already had but were loosing in timing, fuel managment.. even on a stock engine you'll gain hp! <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||1
