Z||000031||Nashco||11-23-2001||12:41 PM||||Alright, I'm going to attempt to help with some stuff here, from MY understanding.  I don't know for sure, but this is what I'm getting out of it.<P>GTMike:  You have to control ignition, fuel, and boost.  An adjustable boost controller makes things even more tuneable.<P>It sounds like this S-AFC can be used instead of the rising rate fuel pressure regulator, or in conjuction with it if some major fuel is needed at high RPM.  You see, the S-AFC can tweak your fuel, but if you have some monster injectors to fuel the motor at high RPM, then you will need to adjust the injectors at idle/low RPM to barely open.  Injectors are picky though, in that they can only open and then close again so fast.  I'm not sure how fast this is, and how much adjustability you will have, so I can't help with much more.  If it did have a ton of fuel demand at high RPM and little at idle, you would probably need the rising rate regulator, but that would make tuning a bit pesky.  Somebody else will have to help with this.<P>Also, from what I understand on the Apexi website, the S-AFC is a piggy back computer.  It takes all of your sensor readings, tweaks them, and sends them on their way to your stock computer, which then outputs the injector pulses, etc.  I assume that this, in turn, would also tweak your ignition if you are still using a stock ignition that uses computer inputs.<P>I couldn't tell you what size injectors put out enough for what turbo performance, beyond my knowledge base right now <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR>jimmybpei:  The palm can't tweak your ecm, not our ecm's anyway.  The Palm (Palm Pilot  <A HREF="http://www.palm.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.palm.com</A> ) can plug into the data port that normal scanners would plug into and monitor all of your sensor inputs and some outputs.  Basically, everything your computer is doing that affects the engine is able to be displayed on a scanner.  In the past, any scanner of quality was only affordable for people that were using them a lot to diagnose the car and getting paid for doing so.  Nowadays there are sooo many digital electronics and software tweakers; now there are programs to hook up the palm to the data port and software that interprets all the data.<P>I've been searching the last few days, but still haven't come up with anything for our cars.  I've found a metric buttload of palm programs(windows CE ones too), several automotive scanner programs for OBD II, and zilch for older cars.  Still searching though, give me time, I can find anything on the net if it exists! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Go to <A HREF="http://www.apexi.com" TARGET=_blank>Apexi.com</A> for more info on the Apexi S-AFC.<P>Travis meant that with some massaging (tweaking his settings via chip tuning, fuel pressure regulator adjustments, or S-AFC tuning) the 19# Mustang injectors have worked out better than anything else he's tried in his situation.  His situation is "need to have a hell of a lot of fuel" to match that "got to squirt pounds at a time" nitrous addiction he's got.<P>Shiner:  Yes, the stock mustang units (from years given above in previous posts) will clip right in, no mods needed to the injectors, fuel rails, or wiring clips.  They won't just work right without any other mods though, you do have to tweak your fuel, or the car will run too rich in a stock motor application.  This is not a "cheap fix" alternative, this is a high performance modification, and requires tuning to achieve proper driveability.<P><BR>SO, has anybody else found info on the Palm scanner yet?  Like I said, I've found a ton of OBD II stuff, but can't find anything on pre-OBD II cars yet.  Part of the problem is that most vehicles before OBD II didn't communicate in the same manner, and also shared plug ins, for the most part.  I will keep looking, but a nudge in the right direction would be helpful.<P>I still have a few floating questions, also.  As soon as Trav gets back, he may be able to help with this too(I'll assume he's on vacation).  I'm wondering if, in a NA or turbo application, more fuel pressure is needed in ADDITION to the S-AFC, or if the S-AFC has enough adjustability in it to delete that step.<P>Also, does anybody know where the cheapest reliable fuel pressure regulator and fuel pressure guage can be had?  I know I can find cheap, but I don't know which ones are cheap(er) AND reliable.<P>If SKIDMARK is still reading this thread, I would like those pics if you could get around to it bud <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>For 1FST2M6, was your friend using the palm on an OBD II car?  I am having a tough time finding the software for anything but OBD II, and all at a pretty price compared to what I was expecting (about 300 bucks is the best deal so far, including the input cord).<P>Also, if somebody has this Apexi unit, I'd love to check it out.  I travel a lot between Michigan and Alabama, and I'd go for a few hours of driving to check one of these units out and just hang out in general.  If anybody has one and wants to show and tell, I'd love that <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Ok, this took WAY too long, sorry for the length.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000032||jimmybpei||11-23-2001||08:46 PM||||Nashco- Would the # 19 injectors work well with a highly modifyied motor:  bored out, lots of head work, headers cold air intake bigger t-body & intake bigger cam, roller tip rockers, exhaust 255 l/hr holley fuel pump, regulator wires, coil msd 6al box ect ect oh nx wet nitrous system (same as travis's)<P>If you look at the motor the same as a carbed one, more mod mean more fuel wich means a bigger carb and bigger jets, so shouldn't i need bigger injectors????<P><BR>All this help is confusing<BR>Thanks <BR>Jimmy B.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000033||Nashco||11-24-2001||01:07 AM||||jimmybpei:  Please understand, first off, that I'm learning just as you.  Perhaps I'm following the steep curve a little better, but I'm still learning this as I go just like you <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>From what Travis has said, it seems like the 19# units will feed all the fuel needs any NA 2.8 will need.  He's got some major airflow going with his setup, and the NOS to boot, and is still not demanding too much from the injector.  His setup is even set up well enough that he can use the car as a cruiser, and hit the NOS, all the while using the same fuel curve (right Trav?).  This is hard to do; as he said, he's running rich when not on the juice, but it's well worth it when you need it.<P>Did you know we can use a factory fuel pump to feed all of our needs?  Just makes it nice because you can still use the in-tank pump and have plenty of fuel.  I believe it's an 89 Firebird Anniversary Edition fuel pump that works.<P>1FST2M6:  I re-read the threads, and you DID say that the head-dude hooked up his palm to your stock ECU.  Man, it would be KILLER if you could find out more about his setup!  It seems I'm not the only one interested either <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Hopefully you get in town and check the forum before I go crazy trying to find this stuff online!<P>Oh, I just want to remind anybody/everybody again, I'm no expert on this, just trying to stimulate good conversation and offer my opinion/view of things.  I am getting really interested in all of this, that's for sure.  All of the sudden NOS doesn't seem so far away (after my 3.4, of course <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000034||GTMike||11-24-2001||01:48 AM||||I went to apexi  and read the all about it. they say its just for jap cars.  and they only give wireing diagrams for jap cars.<P>i was thinking of useing the their old modle.  you can ajust how much more or fuel  you want to be added to the engine.   <BR>guess if you had a bad idle for big injectors you could cut he fuel down at the idle rpm ajustment.<P>it would be nice to have a piggy back compter that checks the air/fuel and constantly ajusts the fuel on its own.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Nashco||11-24-2001||02:54 AM||||An interesting point you've brought up.  I DO see in the application section for the S-AFC it says "Vehicle: Universal", but in the instruction manuals it says "for Japanese vehicles only" which makes zero sense.  Looks like I'll sit tight for a few days to hear from Trav about this.  If he doesn't come around before too long maybe I'll email them.<P>I don't understand why you'd want to go with the old module; the new one has way more features.<P>Yes, it would be nice if cars automatically figured out what the best possible set up is, but unfortunately, they can't know that.  You see, you could set up your car many different ways to respond to many different driving conditions.  That's why the factory spends so much time doing calibrations, because they want to get every single thing just right for almost every use of the stock engine.  Sure would be nice if they figured out that I drive hard, want X performance, have X mods, and am not getting enough of X...but it just isn't in reach, yet.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000036||jimmybpei||11-24-2001||07:03 AM||||wouldn't a mass air flow work well with bigger injectors if the computer would pick up the mass. because it(mass) i thinks reads the volume of air entering and ajusts the pulse width accordingly. <P>Not sure may be way off on this one .<P>Nashco- are you saying in my 86 se the fuel pump will deliever more than i wil every demand with the 2.8 and all the mods, if so i think the holley 255 will have to go back so i can better spend that money on more useful parts.<P>Thanks.<BR>Still learning.<BR>Jimmy B.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||FieroNate||11-24-2001||09:39 AM||||I haven't been following the whole post... But i am woring on a swap kit for the 3400 SFI, and also working on a 400 HP naturally asperated 3400 for my project car, I will be runnning a haltec for ignition and fuel mapping (for now) and an MSD DIS-4 to get better the stock spark. My one web page is <A HREF="http://www.easterndynamics.com" TARGET=_blank>www.easterndynamics.com</A> <BR>the other is <A HREF="http://24.229.66.214/~nate/" TARGET=_blank>http://24.229.66.214/~nate/</A> <BR>the first one may not have all the pages linked right <BR>Any questions E-mail <BR>FieroNate@aol.com ||||reg||1
Z||000038||filthyscarecrow||11-24-2001||04:10 PM||||about the SAFC:<BR>from what i understand, it alsohas "high" nad "low" settings, which gives you 2 different fuel curves, the juicier curve is activated by a pressure switch to come on when the factory fuel curve just isn't enough. the only issue i see with the japanese cars is that most of them use sequential port injection, where ours just fires one bank of injectors at a time. sequential injects just before the intake stroke on each individual cylinder. <BR>i don't think this would be an issue though because the AFC doesn't know the difference really, it only modifies the inj. pulse width coming from the stock ECM depending on RPM. so the ECM is still firing the injectors, the AFC is just intercepting that signal and modifying it to hold the inj. open longer. it would think we're just running a really big 2 cyl. engine. should be good to go... <P>i'm currently installing a turbo on my 86SE, and i'm goign with the 19# injectors ($26/8 on ebay, i plan to flow test them when i get them...) but i'm also having a custom PROM burned for the application. i want to know about the palm pilot thing because i would like a way to monitor things for the first few drives to make sure nothing is horribly wrong. air/fuel guages are not accurate enough in my opinion. i saw on turbobrick website that one can just splice in a digital voltmeter to the O2 sensor to monitor lambda in real time. anyone tried this on the fiero?<P>also, i've seen some discussion about ideal fuel ratios. yes, 14.7:1 is stoich, but max power is made at 12.6:1 (richer than stoich). if you're trying to lean it out to 14.7 at WOT (100% load), you're running dangerously close to lean adn if something changes abruptly, yuo'd better get out your checkbook. i believe the lambda reading for max power is like 0.85 (voltmeter) and stoich is 1.0. if yuo're running above 1.0, you're running too lean. that's what i gathered hepling map the R6 using MoTeC last spring. hope it helps...<BR>if anyone does manage to find the info on the palm pilot, let me know. email me, message me, whatever. thanks...<p>[This message has been edited by filthyscarecrow (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000039||Nashco||11-24-2001||07:23 PM||||Jimmy, I emailed you some info on the 89 Firebird fuel pump.<P>The MAF (mass air flow) and MAP sensor would be ideal, but our computers don't have an input for MAF as far as I know.<P>FieroNate, you may want to read the whole thread, as it has info about what the forum members have had good experiences with.  It seems that Haltec isn't the best way to go.<P>Just in case anybody is seriously ready to put the cash down on an S-AFC unit right now: <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=598277991" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=598277991</A> <P>scarecrow:  I never got that about the "switchable" settings, where are you getting that idea from?  I did get that there are variable fuel levels based on RPM and throttle position.  The web page talks about this in the "Hi/Lo Throttle" section.  It will accept adjustments at 8 RPM levels, and interpolate everything in between.<P>One of is us mistaken; I don't think that it has any switchable fuel curve, only uses what you've programmed into its SINGLE curve.  That's the entire point of the box is that you can completely adjust the entire fuel curve, very little dependency on the factory fuel curve.<P>Also, as you said, the Apexi unit doesn't know anything about the firing method of the injectors, it doesn't even know what injectors are.  The S-AFC just modifies your readings from the sensors to "fake" the conditions that would give huge amounts of fuel or little fuel, depending on what you needed.<P>With the S-AFC, you shouldn't need a custom PROM burned; this makes you have an adjustable PROM sort of...you can tweak everything involved with the fuel curve without fiddling with your prom.  There are some things you can do with the PROM that the Apexi has nothing to do with; like making it accept 2 Bar inputs, adjust idle RPM, battery voltage dependent idle, etc.<P>You're right about running rich (with a turbo)...you always want to be on the rich side rather than perfect or lean with a turbo.<P>I don't know much about just watching the O2 sensor.  I am on the DIY-EFI email list, where I've obtained some Wideband O2 sensor stuff.  Rather than having a limited area to monitor (with a standard O2 sensor) the wideband O2 sensor measure a very broad air/fuel spectrum, and is also heated.  I haven't got the parts yet, but I am positive this will be better to monitor engine performance than splicing a voltmeter into the factory O2 sensor.  Basically, our standard O2 sensor has limits; it will tell you if you're rich or lean, but doesn't have much range to tell you.  The wideband will tell you exactly how rich or lean from approximately 10:1 to 17:1, I believe.<P>terryk is surely on the DIY EFI list, and knows more about this than I do.  Hopefully if he reads this and I'm way off, he'll correct me.<P>filthyscarecrow:  If we find something out on the palm, it'll be posted here.  I'm not trying to be an a-hole, but nobody is going to go out of their way to message you about it.  Just check up on the thread as you have been.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000040||filthyscarecrow||11-24-2001||08:38 PM||||well, the info i got on the AFC is from 2 different guys, one of which uses one on his 400HP twin turbo supra, the other is going to use one on his 300HP DSM turbo, so i guess i would trust them as fas as knowing how it works (the supra guy is the one who built his own air&gt;h2o intercooler, he knows his stuff). the reason being for 2 different curves is so that gobs of fuel can be dumped in at high RPM/load while still being able to idle like a civilised machine and drive the same under normal conditions. he said you can either use a throttle switch (set at, say, 85%throttle), or a pressure switch so it dumps in more fuel at a specified boost level. chances are we're both thinking the same thing, just describing it differently. <BR>as for fuel ratio, either way you want to run slightly rich any time the engine is under load. this applies equally to both turbo and NA engines. stoich is great for emissions and mileage on the highway, but for power you need to run rich. it has to do with the fact that under load, fuel is an excellent coolant. under load is when the engine is going to run hottest, adn running richer provides cooling of combustion chamber temps. that's kind of what the EGR system is all about- not only does it recycle some unburned fuel, but it lowers the combustion chamber temp to reduce the chance of burning a hole through your pistons during fast driving. like i said before though, you won't make max power until 12.6:1 anyway, so why even bother shooting for stoichiometric mixture under load? <BR>and i don't think you were trying to be an *******  about anything, but if i knew something someone else wanted to know and they asked me to email them about it, i sure as hell would. i thought that's what this forum was all about... you don't have to email me, but i hope someone who knows something would, as that is something i'm very interested in. ||||reg||1
Z||000041||Nashco||11-24-2001||09:36 PM||||Yeah, we're basically on the same page.  How you're describing a "switch" is really a sensor.  Like how you explained the 85% throttle, that's part of the "Hi/Lo Throttle" settings in the S-AFC.<P>I'm not disagreeing about running rich.  It is known, as you said, that running a little rich will cool the cylinders better.  As far as making max power at a specific value (12.6:1) I think this will vary with every engine.  Along the way I've seen all sorts of numbers for best results, which makes me believe this number is different for every car.<P>scarecrow:  Have you found any stuff on the net for palm scanners?  I haven't been able to find anything that's pre-OBD II yet.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000042||GTMike||11-24-2001||10:45 PM||||the reason i was thinking of the old one i cause  the new one seems so darn complicated.  and the site did not offer the diagrams for any gm car.    so i would not know how to set all the switches on the back of the afc.  but if i could get accurate info on how to hook it up to the gm wireing harness i would get the newn one. maybe we should to it as a group....   now we just need some cross over pipes.  maybe i'll  have to break down and have to have a shop make me  one. ||||reg||1
Z||000043||terryk||11-24-2001||11:14 PM||||"The MAF (mass air flow) and MAP sensor would be ideal, but our computers don't have an input for MAF as far as I know."<P>So try the 6870 ECM from 85. The pin out is nearly identical and supports mass air.<P>Turns out that the 85 Chevy V6 MPFI got certified with the mass and the Fiero got certified with map. Each were backup's should one fail federal certification. The Fiero could have gotten the mass system if the SD failed and vice-versa.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000044||Nashco||11-24-2001||11:53 PM||||So terryk, I can assume the "6870 ECM from 85" is for the Chevy, or no?  Also, I like that the 88 doesn't have an RPM limiter, is there a way to bypass that in 85 without custom burning the PROM?  Also, does it allow BOTH the MAF and MAP, or just the MAF?  I'm thinking really hard...and thinking you can't have MAF all on it's own, but then again, my brain is known to think wrong <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>GTMike: that's kind of what this is, trying to scavenge information from several sources to get a good solid upgrade solution.  I didn't see support for any GM vehicles on the first gen or second gen model of the Apexi unit, so either one is fine for me.  I know hooking up wires is easy enough, it's the voltage values that bother me.  I'm not sure if the Apexi unit modifies voltage, or if it takes an input and makes an output based on the Japanese vehicle's values.  I know some sensors are the same across many vehicle platforms, but some are vehicle dependent.  If they were smart about it (and I'm assuming they were) they've made it so that it just tweaks your values so it could be used on any vehicle.  We'll see what Trav has to say when he gets back around, I suppose.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>P.S.  Thanks for everybody's help so far, this is fantastic knowledge and very good brain workout material for me! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000045||filthyscarecrow||11-25-2001||02:42 AM||||the closest thing i've seen so far to the palm is the ALDL adapter for the laptop. i'll probably go with that if i can't find the palm. we've got a craptop that we use for data acquisition from the formula car, i'm sure they wno't mind if i borrow it for a while...||||reg||1
Z||000046||terryk||11-25-2001||02:50 AM||||The 6870 is used in any W and S code engines in 85 (2.8L V6 MPFI) and the V8 TPI's. No map, there was no provisions for a map backup. Mass only.<P>I've run both and both have advantages and disadvantages. The masses are very touchy.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000047||Nashco||11-25-2001||02:59 AM||||terryk, what would you say were the most important advantages and disadvantages?  Like you said, the MAF is touchy.  I've also had lots of experiences with older designed MAF sensors going bad often.  I've never had many MAP sensors go bad...but they don't seem to be as good for performance applications.  I'm not sure on the reasoning, that's something I'll have to look up.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000048||terryk||11-25-2001||12:00 PM||||From a practical standpoint, the mass systems are more tolerant of changes. They read air flow directly and are more accurate (usually) in fuel delivery. The downside is that the masses dislike disruptions in airflow and vibrations. Masses can get expensive.<P>Speed density is simpler and cheaper, but less tolerate of engine changes.<P>With really wild cams, boths systems will start to give driveability problems.<P>Someone should try the mass system in a Fiero. I tried a Fiero SD in my 85 X-11 and found that it didn't like my H260 cam, but that was before I got farther into it.<P>The 6870 ECM and 6869 ECM are the same hardware with different processor code to make them SD or Mass. <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000049||jimmybpei||11-25-2001||07:22 PM||||Nashc - i have the holley fuel pump already so i guess i will install it just to be sure that i am gett the fuel to the motor. <P>another que tho: is the fuel rail in the 2.8 large enough and has anyone had problems in this area with fuel delivery. because it doesn't matter how much you can pump it, it is still only as fast as it smallest restriction.<P>Found the stang #19 injectors for free(lots of mustang guys around here).<P>Later <BR>Jimmy B.||||reg||1
Z||000050||Nashco||11-25-2001||11:10 PM||||terryk:  Sounds like I'll stick with the MAP sensor then.  Like you said, MAF sensors get expensive, and a MAP will get what I need, until a turbo maybe later down the road.  Then again, there are plenty of disruptions in that airflow setup.  Food for thought in my future setup, I suppose.<P>jimmybpei:  I can't imagine the 2.8 fuel rail could ever be more restrictive than our intakes.  Luckily the fuel system is already a forced induction system, so to get more flow you can just allow more fuel pressure through and adjust the injectors accordingly.  If your motor is ever desiring that much fuel though, something will self destruct real quick though, so probably not an issue <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>So, you have a hook up on any more free injectors???? (not enough mustang guys around here, evidently <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000051||jimmybpei||11-26-2001||03:45 PM||||Nashco- if i come accross any more free #19 ijectors i'll let you know.<BR>Shouldn't be that big of a problem everyone here seems to be swaping them out.<P>My fuel system sould be fine then, holley fuel pump, adj. fuel reg, #19 ijectors. That sould do it rite ????<P>Now onto the ignition system MSD 6AL box MSD coil, need new wires??? ngk ur5's spark plugs (travis advice) What else???<P><BR>Thanks for the info <BR>Jimmy B. ||||reg||1
Z||000052||Nashco||11-27-2001||01:36 AM||||Well Jimmy, that's kind of what this whole thread is about.  Lots of people just use adjustable fuel pressure regulator and larger injectors, but that doesn't give optimum performance.  If you want to optimize fuel usage, you have to either custom program your PROM or get something like the Apexi unit.  Yes, you can do without them, but you will have little appreciable gains...you will get the car to not run horrible, but will have little to no added performance.  That's what I am getting out of the whole deal, anyway.<P>As far as ignition goes, if you're getting an MSD ignition and coil, why not new hi-po wires at the same time?  No sense in bottle-necking your ignition with crappy plugs, wires, etc.<P>Hopefully Travis shows up soon...his knowledge sure would be helpful on a couple of these things.  I remember him recommending those NGK plugs, I bet if you did a search you could check that...but it's likely he'll show up soon and could verify it.<P>I'd be completely thankful if you scored me a set of those injectors, that'd make experimentation/tuning a lot easier <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000053||Nashco||11-27-2001||10:04 PM||||Travis...1FST2M6...we had a couple questions along the way for you...would you mind helpin' us out on some of this stuff???<P>Specifically, wondering what program your friend is using on the Palm for the Fiero, confirming the Apexi will work with our ECM, and which plugs to use with NOS.  Thanks!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
